 { Now we will cover one example together, to give you an idea of the kind of puzzles you might expect. This puzzle is medium difficulty. White has a good position here, with the e5 pawn frustrating Black's piece activity. Can you find the most precise way of increasing the advantage? } 1. { -KEY- } Ng5 $1 { Correct! The point of this move is that Black now has nothing better than to play ...g6, when their dark squares will be permanently weakened. } g6 { Here, White can play it safe with a move like Rf1-e1, or they can be more aggressive and sacrifice the pawn for immediate play against the newly created weak squares on f6 and h6. } 2. Ne4 $5 Nxe5 { Black has to accept the sacrifice. With the e5 pawn on the board, Black's weakness on f6 was an outpost and this alone was too much to manage. } 3. Bh6 Re8 4. Ne3 $1 { A beautiful move! White challenges the Black knight, as it sits on the d5 light square from where it covers the important dark square on f6. Black is already lost, for example: } Nxe3 5. fxe3 $1 f5 6. Rad1 Qe7 7. Bg5 $1 { The killer move. } Qg7 8. Nf6+ $18 Kf8 9. Nxe8 Kxe8 10. Qc3 $18 { Black's weak king and poor piece coordination means that further material losses are inevitable. An important note before you begin: the puzzles will not require you to solve such long variations. In many cases, the puzzle will end once you have found the key strategic idea. In this case, the puzzle itself would end after 1.Ng5! (Please set the course study mode to 'key moves' instead of 'all moves' if you haven't already). The rest of the annotation would be included with the goal of helping the student understand each position more deeply. I hope this test example has been helpful, and good luck in the puzzle set! } 
 *

