 { Black to play. Can you identify where the weak squares lie and find a move that neutralizes the danger? } 1... { -KEY- } Qf4 $1 { Correct! This takes away access to the weak h6 square for White's queen, defusing any attacking possibilities. It also has the benefit of a double attack against White's queen and knight. } ( 1... h5 $2 { If Black underestimates the danger, they would meet a textbook-like finish: } 2. Qh6 $1 Kg8 3. Re5 $1 hxg4 4. Rg5 { The invasion along the dark squares leaves Black defenseless. } Nf4 ( 4... Ne7 { This way of defending the g6 pawn meets a brutal end. } 5. Rh5 $3 gxh5 6. Bh7 Kh8 7. Bg6 Kg8 8. Qh7 Kf8 9. Qxf7 ) 
5. Rxg4 Nxd3 { Black captures the bishop, since otherwise White will recover their piece and have an easily winning position. } 6. Rh4 { Now White has forced mate. } f6 7. Qh8 Kf7 8. Rh7 ) 
2. Qxf4 $2 { In the game, it was White who went wrong. This move allowed Black's minor pieces to become too active. } ( 2. Qe2 { White's best move would be to step the queen away from the diagonal. Here, Black could play ...Rad8, or even drop the queen back with ...Qc7. In either case, the position remains equal. } ) 
Nxf4 3. Bc4 { The only way of avoiding material losses. } ( 3. Bc2 $2 Ba6 $19 ) 
c5 $17 { Black is the one with a strong initiative, due to the double attack against the d4 and g2 pawns. } 
