 { White's pawn on e4 is under fire and, at the same time, Black has the option of damaging White's structure on the queenside with ... Bxc3. On the other hand, Black's dark squares appear to be very weak. How should White continue? } 1. { -KEY- } e5 $16 { Correct! White doesn't have to fear ...Bxc3+ since the damage to their structure will be compensated by the fact that Black will have parted ways with their powerful dark-squared bishop. At the same time, by playing e4-e5, White has now gained an outpost on the important central square on d6. Such kinds of decisions, where one side allows damage to their structure in exchange for dominion over a color complex, are very common in major chess opening systems. One example that comes to mind is the Winawer variation of the French Defense. } 
